# ── Line endings for Node script modules ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# The repo uses core.autocrlf=true, so a fresh clone on Windows gets CRLF in the
# working tree. That is harmless for the site and for the functions, but NOT for
# the Node ESM scripts under scripts/: vitest's transform fails on a CRLF .mjs
# with a bare "SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token" and no location — while
# Node itself imports the exact same file without complaint.
#
# Reproduced 2026-09-12 on scripts/ci/verify-env.mjs imported by
# netlify/functions/_lib/verify-env-budget.test.ts: identical content failed with
# CRLF and passed with LF. (Not caused by non-ASCII characters — stripping them
# made no difference — and not by import.meta; both were tested.)
#
# Pinning these to LF makes it deterministic instead of depending on how the
# working tree happened to be checked out. Git already stores them as LF, so
# this changes nothing about the committed content.
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.cjs text eol=lf

# ── Shell scripts, for a different and sharper reason ────────────────────────
#
# A CRLF shell script is already wrong on principle, but the concrete damage here
# is worse than a stray `\r` in a message. The mutation batteries drive `node -e`
# helpers whose OLD/NEW anchors are multi-line strings passed through the
# ENVIRONMENT, and the helper asserts "exactly one match" before writing. A CRLF
# working tree makes every such anchor miss:
#
#     $ MUT_OLD='  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then\n    echo "KILLED ..."\n  else' \
#       node -e '... s.split(o).length - 1 ...'
#     hits: 0
#
# Measured 2026-09-15 on scripts/ci/check-md-tables.mutations.sh, which
# `git ls-files --eol` reported as `i/lf w/crlf` — the index holds LF and the
# working tree held CRLF, precisely because this file is NOT matched by the two
# patterns above and `core.autocrlf=true` rewrote it on checkout.
#
# The good case is a hard ABORT (`hits=0 -> exit 3`), which is loud. The bad case
# is any helper that does NOT assert: `str.replace()` matches nothing, the file is
# written back unchanged, the gate correctly stays green, and the battery reports
# SURVIVED — indistinguishable from a real hole in the gate. That is the exact
# failure this repository already documents as its most expensive trap, and EOL
# is one of the ways to fall into it.
#
# Pinned for the same reason as the .mjs files: the committed bytes are LF either
# way, so this only removes a source of local-only, checkout-dependent failure.
*.sh text eol=lf
